## Container queries changed component design

Media queries ask about the viewport. Components don't live in the viewport — they live in a slot whose width you don't control.

```css
.card-wrapper { container-type: inline-size; }

@container (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card { grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr; }
}
```

This is the difference between a component that works and a component that works *where you put it*. Before container queries, the usual workaround was a size prop threaded down from a parent that had to know about layout — coupling that never survived a redesign.

## View transitions replace a category of animation code

Animating between two DOM states used to mean keeping both alive simultaneously and orchestrating the handoff by hand.

```js
document.startViewTransition(() => {
  updateTheDOM();
});
```

The browser snapshots before and after, then cross-fades between them. Named transitions let individual elements morph rather than fade, which is the effect people were writing hundreds of lines of code to approximate.

The constraint worth knowing early: the callback must update the DOM synchronously. Anything async has to resolve before you start the transition, which changes how you sequence data fetching.

## Types at the boundary, inference inside

The common failure is annotating everything. Explicit types on every local variable add noise and, worse, prevent inference from catching a genuine mismatch.

Annotate where values enter and leave: function parameters, public return types, parsed external data. Let inference handle the middle. The compiler is better at it than you are and it updates when you refactor.

```ts
// Boundary — annotate
export function parseOrder(raw: unknown): Order { ... }

// Interior — infer
const totals = orders.map(o => o.lines.reduce((s, l) => s + l.amount, 0));
```

## Discriminated unions eliminate whole bug classes

A type with optional fields for every variant permits states that can't exist.

```ts
// Permits { loading: true, error: Error, data: Order }
type State = { loading?: boolean; error?: Error; data?: Order };

// Permits exactly three states
type State =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'error'; error: Error }
  | { status: 'ready'; data: Order };
```

The second form makes the impossible states unrepresentable, and `switch` over `status` gets exhaustiveness checking for free. Most "why is data undefined here" bugs are the first form.

## Error responses need a shape

Errors are part of the contract and they're routinely designed last, which is why they're the least consistent part of most APIs.

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "insufficient_stock",
    "message": "Only 3 units of SKU-4471 remain",
    "field": "items[2].quantity",
    "retryable": false
  }
}
```

The `code` is for programs and must be stable. The `message` is for humans and may change. Conflating them means clients string-match on prose, and your copy edit becomes their outage.

## Motion needs a reduced-motion path

`prefers-reduced-motion` isn't a niche preference — for people with vestibular disorders, a parallax scroll is genuinely disabling.

The implementation mistake is disabling all animation, which often breaks the interface: a menu that animated open now appears with no indication of where it came from.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; }
}
```

This blunt version is better than nothing and worse than designing the reduced state. Replace movement with a cross-fade rather than removing the transition entirely.
